					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607547072" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1466571410 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Jun 2016 04:56</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2527113">
									

<p>Somewhat conflicted on this one. On the one hand, I definitely enjoy the story being told, and find the characters and suggested background quite engaging in the small glimpses seen. There's some clever story-telling here.</p>
<p>On the other hand&#8230;. mixing the elements of major reality bending, the wooden box, and Alzheimer's disease feels by having him spontaneously create extremely convenient flashbacks feels&#8230; cheap, fake-y, and, well, overly convenient. Sort of&#8230; Alzheimer's can have much more drastic symptoms, and -1 is basically a god within the box, but somehow that <em>only</em> translates into producing these occasional neat little vignettes. It doesn't help that the old man and box contents seem so Dickensian, which feels slightly fake-y.</p>
<p>I might feel better if there were an indication that he occupies his time with something in the box (one would assume writing), so there's a reason he's not just recreating yesteryear on a much more regular basis and with much less context.</p>
<p>I'm no-voting for now, I might reread it later.</p>

							</div>

								
